Abstract—Increased GHG emissions from human activities have severe environmental and health impacts, making accurate predictions essential for effective mitigation strategies. This review explores various models for GHG emission prediction, focusing on ML DL techniques. It examines the contributions of sectors such as transportation, agriculture, and energy production, and discusses challenges in estimating emissions from sources like soils, livestock, and hydroelectric power. Prediction accuracy has significantly increased thanks to developments in ML and DL algorithms, such as RNNs, CNNs, and artificial neural networks (ANNs). Case studies highlight the effectiveness of these models in capturing complex emission patterns and informing emission reduction decisions. Integrating ML and DL with traditional environmental data analysis offers a comprehensive approach to GHG emission prediction, supporting sustainable development and climate change mitigation. Despite significant progress, further research is needed to enhance model interpretability, data integration, and real-time prediction capabilities to address the dynamic nature of GHG emissions and their global impact.
Keywords—Greenhouse Gas Emission, Emission Trends, Predictive Modeling
I. INTRODUCTION
GHGs like ozone, nitrous oxide, chlorofluorocarbons, methane and carbon dioxide play a crucial role in absorbing infrared radiation. This process traps heat in the atmosphere, leading to global warming. The Earth's temperature has been on a steady rise over the last century [1]. Global warming results in heat-related health issues, like heat strokes, which can lead to premature deaths, especially during the summer months. Greenhouse gases (GHGs) function like a partial blanket, trapping heat by radiating long-wave radiation and absorbing warmth from the Earth's surface. Human activities, particularly since the Industrial Revolution, have significantly increased the emissions of major GHGs, such as CO2, CH4, N2O, and O3 [2]. Many of these gases persist in the atmosphere for a long time, which makes their impacts almost impossible to reverse.A significant challenge to achieving sustainable development and creating greener cities is the issue of greenhouse gas emissions [3]. Human activities, especially in transportation, play a major role in contributing to greenhouse gas emissions. In response, various alternative fuels and powertrains have been developed for road transportation to help reduce these emissions [4]. The expanding airline industry, characterized by crowded air routes, leads to delays, increased fuel consumption, and higher
greenhouse gas emissions [5]. The automobile industry also uses substantial quantities of gasoline and electricity [6]. Minimizing the environmental impact of transportation systems is essential [7].Agriculture plays a significant role in greenhouse gas emissions. Changes in land use and farming practices, especially in livestock production, release methane [8]. Manure management on dairy farms, particularly the use of liquid manure, can lead to an increase in greenhouse gas emissions [9]. While hydroelectric power is often seen as a renewable energy source, it can still lead to greenhouse gas emissions due to the decay of vegetation and emissions associated with reservoirs [10]. Even after decades of research, predicting and reducing greenhouse gas emissions accurately continues to be a significant challenge in the realm of sustainable development.
