B. Harnessing AI: Deep Learning and Machine Learning for Predicting Green House Gas Emissions
Machine learning and deep learning, two subfields of artificial intelligence, have transformed a variety of industries by allowing computers to learn from data and make decisions on their own without explicit programming[19]. Machine learning algorithms recognize patterns in extensive datasets, whereas deep learning models emulate brain functions to handle intricate information [20]. Machine learning is divided into three basic categories: unsupervised, supervised, and reinforcement learning. Unsupervised learning focuses on discovering patterns in unlabeled data, whereas supervised learning uses labeled data to train the model. [21]. Training agents to make a series of decisions based on a reward system is Reinforcement learning.Deep learning emphasizes the use of artificial neural networks that consist of interconnected layers of data-processing nodes. It performs exceptionally well in areas such as NLP, image recognition, and autonomous driving [22, 23]. While recurrent neural networks (RNNs) are excellent at processing sequential input [24]. AI is crucial for estimating greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions, especially through machine learning and deep learning. It analyzes large datasets obtained from sources such as satellite images, climate models, and sensor networks [25]. These algorithms can pinpoint emission hotspots, monitor changes over time, and offer insights for focused emission reduction strategies [26]. By examining aspects like energy use, transportation, and land management, AI can assist researchers and policymakers in creating effective strategies for mitigation. Machine learning models can also forecast future emissions and identify early indicators of highemission activities, such as deforestation or industrial outputs, which aids in enhancing efforts to reduce emissions[27, 28].
C. Unleashing the Power of Deep Learning and Machine Learning for Green House Gas Emissions
Machine learning (ML), which is a branch of AI, utilizes regression, classification, and prediction models that achieve
high accuracy in various industries thanks to its robust capabilities and effective training methods. As computational power continues to grow, statistical techniques are being more frequently employed to examine intricate environmental issues [29]. For example, machine learning models were used to assess nitrous oxide (N2O) and carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions over a five-year period in Quebec, Canada. In Turkey, various methods such as SVM, DL, and Artificial Neural Networks (ANN) were employed to forecast greenhouse gas emissions, with the DL model delivering particularly accurate outcomes [30].
III. NAVIGATING THE STORM : OVERCOMING CHALLENGES IN ML AND DL FOR GREEN HOUSE GAS PREDICTION
Accurate predictions of greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions are essential for tackling climate change, yet ML and DL models encounter various challenges. These models need substantial amounts of labeled data to reach high levels of accuracy, but obtaining this data for GHG forecasts can be both costly and time-consuming. Using previously trained models from related jobs is known as transfer learning, although it might not adjust effectively to data unique to greenhouse gas emissions.GHG emissions are affected by a variety of factors, it can be challenging to identify relevant features from complex datasets, and this often requires specialized knowledge, particularly when accounting for time-dependent elements like seasonal variations and shifts in policy. Models that rely on historical data might find it challenging to forecast emissions in new situations because of changes in technology, regulations, or socioeconomic factors. This highlights the need for ongoing updates to ensure accurate predictions.Deep neural networks are great at spotting intricate patterns, but their "black-box" nature brings up issues regarding interpretability, particularly in important decision-making scenarios. Finding a balance between model complexity and interpretability continues to be a challenge in greenhouse gas predictions. Moreover, issues with data availability and quality, like missing values or inconsistencies, can hinder the performance of machine learning and deep learning models, making it even more challenging to forecast greenhouse gas emissions accurately.
