Wei Sun and associates created a model for predicting CO2 emissions that combines particle swarm optimization for least squares support vector machine and principal component analysis (PCA). Their research, carried out in Hebei province, China, from 1990 to 2016, utilized PCA to reduce 24 important factors into two principal components. The PSOLSSVM model achieved a prediction error of only 0.663%, surpassing traditional models like BPNN and LSSVM [25].
Igor and colleagues employed the SVM-FFA method to estimate CO2 emissions about population growth within the European Union. By analyzing data from the World Bank, they discovered that the SVM-FFA model yielded highly accurate predictions, achieving RMSE and R-squared values of 4.2260 and 0.9015, respectively, based on gaseous fuel consumption [13].
