C. Summary of state of the art work related to Greenhouse Gas Emissions Prediction using Long short -term memory network (LSTM)
Xianfu et al. sought to effectively monitor and estimate carbon emissions using the LSTM-trained SVR model demonstrated strong predictive capabilities, especially in forecasting elements such as carbon emissions and labor productivity in the "Yangtze River Economic Belt" basin [26]. A case study conducted by Jun and colleagues in Anhui, China, demonstrated that this method achieved a 35.21% reduction in RMSE for three pollutants, resulting in an RMSE of 8.15 [45]. Chan et al. created a regression model aimed at predicting carbon emissions from coal-fired power plants in China. By analyzing production logs from 2019 to 2021, the model produced MAE, RMSE, MAPE, and R2 values of 289.14, 357.15, 0.021, and 0.842, respectively [47]. Nur et al. predicted GHG emissions from manure management in Indonesia by employing LSTM and GRU models, analyzing data from 1961 to 2021. The models produced RMSE values of 4.25% for cattle, 3.61% for non-dairy cattle, and 3.25% for polluting birds [48].
IV. CONCLUSION
After Understanding climate change, assessing climate models, and helping to limit forecasts of anthropogenic climate change all depend on the detection of climate change and the identification of its causes. In its most recent report, the Intergovernmental Panel on climatic Change (IPCC) makes it abundantly evident that recent global warming is considerable in the context of natural climatic oscillations and that human activities are almost certainly to blame for this climate change. As a result, organizations, decision-makers, and the general public are looking to climate scientists for guidance on what they should do to mitigate dangerous climate change over the long term and adapt to the inevitable further climate change that will occur over the coming decades. To improve the precision and application of ML and DL models, however, a number of concerns, including as data availability and quality, feature selection and engineering, model complexity and interpretability, limited training data, and generalization problems, must be resolved. Interdisciplinary cooperation, standardized data gathering procedures, and additional research on model interpretability and generalization methods are necessary to address these issues.
